@page
@model LarisVMS.Web.Pages.Admin.Settings.DetectionModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "AI detection settings";
}
<h1>Settings</h1>
<partial name="_SettingsTabs" model="@("Detection")" />

@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Model.SavedMessage))
{
    <div class="alert @(Model.StatusIsError ? "alert-danger" : "alert-success")">@Model.SavedMessage</div>
}

<form method="post" class="row g-3" style="max-width: 560px;">
    <div class="col-6">
        <label class="form-label">
            Confidence threshold
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="confidenceHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <div class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
            <input asp-for="Confidence" type="range" min="0" max="1" step="0.05" class="form-range" id="confidenceRange" />
            <output class="text-muted small" style="min-width: 2.5em;" id="confidenceReadout">@Math.Round(Model.Confidence * 100)</output>
        </div>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="confidenceHelp">
            Minimum score a detection needs to be reported at all. Lower catches more real objects but
            also more false positives; higher misses fainter/partial detections. A per-camera override
            is available on that camera's own Edit page.
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-6">
        <label class="form-label">
            IoU threshold
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="iouHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <div class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
            <input asp-for="Iou" type="range" min="0" max="1" step="0.05" class="form-range" id="iouRange" />
            <output class="text-muted small" style="min-width: 2.5em;" id="iouReadout">@Math.Round(Model.Iou * 100)</output>
        </div>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="iouHelp">
            Overlap threshold used to merge duplicate boxes for the same object. A per-camera override
            is available on that camera's own Edit page.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12">
        <label class="form-label">
            Detection model
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="modelFamilyHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <select asp-for="ModelFamily" class="form-select">
            <option value="Auto">Auto (YOLOX)</option>
            <option value="YoloX">YOLOX</option>
            <option value="DFine">D-FINE (experimental)</option>
            <option value="RfDetr" disabled>RF-DETR (not yet available)</option>
        </select>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="modelFamilyHelp">
            Which detection model architecture runs inference. Auto resolves to YOLOX on every
            accelerator — it needs no letterboxing, runs well on low-power and non-Nvidia GPUs, and
            uses an Nvidia GPU fully where present. <strong>D-FINE is experimental</strong> — it works,
            but results can be unpredictable, and objects that straddle two tiles under the Slice
            aspect-fitting option are currently missed or duplicated. Prefer YOLOX unless you are
            specifically evaluating D-FINE. This is a per-node setting, not per-camera: one detection
            process serves every camera on a node from one loaded model — a per-node override is on
            that node's own page (<a asp-page="/Admin/Nodes">manage nodes</a>). YOLOX models are not
            bundled — a node fetches the chosen size from this server on first use.
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-12">
        <label class="form-label">
            YOLOX model size
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="yoloXSizeHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <select asp-for="YoloXSize" class="form-select">
            <option value="Nano">Nano — smallest / fastest (416 input)</option>
            <option value="Tiny">Tiny (416 input)</option>
            <option value="S">S — balanced (default, 640 input)</option>
            <option value="M">M (640 input)</option>
            <option value="L">L (640 input)</option>
            <option value="X">X — largest / most accurate (640 input)</option>
        </select>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="yoloXSizeHelp">
            Only meaningful when the model above resolves to YOLOX. Match it to the node's hardware —
            Nano/Tiny for a low-power box, L/X for a strong GPU. Per-node overridable on
            <a asp-page="/Admin/Nodes">manage nodes</a>. Changing it restarts each camera's detection
            pipeline and fetches the new model.
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-12">
        <label class="form-label">
            D-FINE weights
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="dfineWeightsHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <select asp-for="DFineWeights" class="form-select">
            <option value="Obj2Coco">Obj2Coco (small) — 80 COCO classes (default)</option>
            <option value="Obj2CocoMedium">Obj2Coco (medium) — same 80 classes, heavier/more accurate</option>
            <option value="Obj365">Obj365 (small) — 365 Objects365 classes (richer vocabulary)</option>
        </select>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="dfineWeightsHelp">
            Only meaningful when the detection model above is set to D-FINE. Obj365 recognizes a
            much wider range of specific object types at the cost of a larger, more specific
            vocabulary — see the Snapshots page's own filter tree once it's in use. The medium
            Obj2Coco model keeps the same 80-class vocabulary but uses a larger backbone: more
            accurate, noticeably more work per frame — best paired with a capable GPU and a lower
            detection frame-rate cap. Changing this restarts each camera's detection pipeline.
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-12">
        <label class="form-label">
            D-FINE TensorRT
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="dfineTensorRtHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <select asp-for="DFineTensorRtMode" class="form-select">
            <option value="Off">Off — plain CUDA (default)</option>
            <option value="FP32">FP32 — TensorRT, graph fusion + kernel selection</option>
            <option value="FP16" disabled="@(Model.DFineTensorRtMode != "FP16")">FP16 — not yet available (needs a calibrated model)</option>
        </select>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="dfineTensorRtHelp">
            Only applies when the detection model is D-FINE <em>and</em> the node has TensorRT enabled
            (the machine-local <code>Vision:EnableTensorRt</code> setting) with the TensorRT 10.x SDK
            installed — it does nothing on a node without those. <strong>FP32</strong> runs D-FINE
            through TensorRT for graph fusion and kernel selection with no FP16 math — a safe speedup
            over plain CUDA. <strong>FP16</strong> is not selectable yet: D-FINE is a transformer and a
            straight FP16 cast overflows its decoder (a camera that suddenly reports nothing), and the
            mixed-precision model that would avoid that can't be produced with current tooling. The
            setting and per-node override are in place so FP16 activates automatically once a
            <code>*.fp16.onnx</code> model is bundled. This is a per-node setting; changing it
            recompiles the TensorRT engine, which takes several minutes on the first start per camera
            resolution. A per-node override is on <a asp-page="/Admin/Nodes">manage nodes</a>.
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-12">
        <label class="form-label">
            Aspect fitting
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="aspectModeHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <select asp-for="AspectMode" class="form-select">
            <option value="Letterbox">Letterbox — preserve aspect ratio, pad with black bars (default)</option>
            <option value="Stretch">Stretch — squash to fill the model's square input</option>
            <option value="Slice">Slice — cut into overlapping squares, run each at full resolution</option>
        </select>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="aspectModeHelp">
            How a camera's own real aspect ratio fits into the detection model's square input.
            Letterbox keeps the model seeing correct proportions for a portrait or panoramic
            camera, at the cost of some black bar padding; Stretch distorts a non-square camera's
            geometry to fill the square exactly (this app's behavior before this setting existed).
            Slice scales the short edge to the model's own input size and cuts the long edge into
            2 or more overlapping squares, each detected at full resolution and merged back into
            one result — the best option for catching small or distant objects on a wide or tall
            camera, at the cost of roughly N&times; the inference work (a 16:9 camera runs 2
            detections per frame instead of 1). Requires GPU frame preprocessing — there is no CPU
            fallback for slicing, so it forces that setting on for any camera using it regardless of
            the toggle below. Lower the max detection frame rate if this pushes drop% up on the
            cadence log. This is a per-node setting, not per-camera — a per-node override is
            available on that node's own page (<a asp-page="/Admin/Nodes">manage nodes</a>).
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12">
        <label class="form-label">
            Detection stream
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="streamRoleHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <select asp-for="StreamRole" class="form-select">
            <option value="Sub">Sub</option>
            <option value="Main">Main</option>
        </select>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="streamRoleHelp">
            Which of a camera's streams AI detection actually watches — Sub (lower resolution, lighter
            load) by default. A per-camera override is available on that camera's own Edit page.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12">
        <label class="form-label">
            Camera orientation
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="orientationHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <select asp-for="Orientation" class="form-select">
            <option value="Auto">Auto</option>
            <option value="Landscape">Landscape</option>
            <option value="Portrait">Portrait</option>
        </select>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="orientationHelp">
            Auto trusts the resolution a camera reports for its detection stream. Some corridor-mounted
            cameras advertise a landscape stream (704x480) while actually sending portrait video
            (480x704) — detection then runs on a squashed frame and snapshot crops come out
            horizontally stretched. Setting Portrait corrects the shape before the frame reaches the
            model. Almost always a per-camera setting rather than a deployment default; the override
            is on that camera's own Edit page.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12">
        <label class="form-label">
            Idle timeout (seconds)
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="idleTimeoutHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <input asp-for="IdleTimeoutSeconds" type="number" min="0" class="form-control" required />
        <div class="help-text-source" id="idleTimeoutHelp">
            How long a detected object can go unseen (or sit idle) before its snapshot is finalized and
            a later re-detection starts a new one. Too short fragments one continuous sighting into
            several snapshots; too long delays finalizing a snapshot after the object is actually gone.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12">
        <label class="form-label">
            Max detection frame rate
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="maxFpsHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <input asp-for="MaxFps" type="number" min="0" max="60" class="form-control" required />
        <div class="help-text-source" id="maxFpsHelp">
            The ceiling on how many frames per second per camera reach the detection model. The
            recorder still decodes each camera's Sub stream in real time — this just drops the surplus
            before inference so the GPU idles between frames instead of running flat out (which is
            what causes thermal throttling on a busy node). It has no effect on a camera whose Sub
            stream is already at or below this rate. 0 disables the cap. 10 is plenty for object
            tracking; raise it only if fast motion is being missed. Per-node overridable on
            <a asp-page="/Admin/Nodes">manage nodes</a>.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12 form-check">
        <input asp-for="ReportIdleDetections" type="checkbox" class="form-check-input" />
        <label asp-for="ReportIdleDetections" class="form-check-label">
            Report idle (stationary) objects
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="reportIdleHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="reportIdleHelp">
            Off by default — a track that stops moving no longer keeps its snapshot open, so a parked
            vehicle or a person standing still doesn't get reported at all once it settles. Turn this on
            to also report objects that are present but not moving.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12 form-check">
        <input asp-for="SnapshotMotionAccuracy" type="checkbox" class="form-check-input" />
        <label asp-for="SnapshotMotionAccuracy" class="form-check-label">
            Snapshot motion accuracy
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="snapshotMotionAccuracyHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="snapshotMotionAccuracyHelp">
            On by default. Two related improvements: a stationary object whose detection box wobbles
            slightly frame-to-frame (a parked car at a distance) no longer counts as "moving" and
            stops producing stray snapshots; and once a moving object leaves the scene its snapshot is
            finalized within a few seconds instead of staying open long enough for a later, unrelated
            object of the same type to be folded into it. Turn it off to compare against the previous
            behaviour. Global; changing it restarts each camera's detection pipeline.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12 form-check">
        <input asp-for="GpuPreprocessing" type="checkbox" class="form-check-input" />
        <label asp-for="GpuPreprocessing" class="form-check-label">
            GPU frame preprocessing
            <button type="button" class="help-icon" data-help-target="gpuPreprocessingHelp" tabindex="0" aria-label="Help">ℹ️</button>
        </label>
        <div class="help-text-source" id="gpuPreprocessingHelp">
            Off by default. Moves the per-frame colour conversion and normalization that detection
            does before every inference off the CPU and onto the accelerator (it becomes part of the
            model graph, so it runs on NVIDIA, AMD or Intel hardware the same way). Meant to lower CPU
            load on nodes running several cameras. This is a per-node setting; toggling it restarts
            each camera's detection pipeline. Forced on automatically for a camera using the Slice
            aspect fitting option above, regardless of this toggle — there is no CPU fallback for
            frame slicing.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-12">
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary"><span aria-hidden="true">💾</span> Save</button>
    </div>
</form>

@section Scripts {
    <script>
        // Confidence/IoU slider readouts, wired explicitly by id rather than the previous
        // oninput="this.nextElementSibling..." adjacency coupling — that read as structurally
        // correct on paper (input immediately followed by its own <output>) but didn't update while
        // dragging in practice. Explicit ids sidestep whatever the adjacency assumption was actually
        // getting wrong, and initializing from the slider's own value below (rather than trusting
        // the two to already agree) rules out the readout and the slider ever silently disagreeing.
        (function () {
            function wireSlider(rangeId, readoutId) {
                var range = document.getElementById(rangeId);
                var readout = document.getElementById(readoutId);
                if (!range || !readout) return;
                function sync() { readout.textContent = Math.round(range.value * 100); }
                range.addEventListener('input', sync);
                sync(); // in case the slider's own value differs from what was rendered server-side
            }
            wireSlider('confidenceRange', 'confidenceReadout');
            wireSlider('iouRange', 'iouReadout');
        })();
    </script>
}
